// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/pln102-c93-par-captured-ref.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/4 -> 0/4 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/4 -> 0/4 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @C93 — a par worker may only read captured parent state.
//
// @PLN102 C93 (read residual) — a par worker runs on an ISOLATED store clone, so a
// captured REFERENCE argument (vector/struct/…) is invalid in the worker's store.
// It used to read empty (silent-wrong) or, mis-ordered, slot-panic in codegen. Now a
// clean COMPILE error (no runtime errors, ever — disallow what cannot work). Captured
// SCALARS cross by value; the loop ELEMENT may be a reference (see 22-threading, whose
// `const Score` element still works). Pass tests: 22-threading + scalar-context cases.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: captured argument 'cap' is a reference
// @EXPECT_ERROR: captured argument 's' is a reference
// The mis-ordered case reports through loft#1060's pair, which replaced the narrower kind
// test that used to stand here.  Both are true of `misordered(c, x)` and each is separately
// actionable: the argument written first is not the element, AND the parameter it would
// land in cannot take one.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: its first argument is the loop element
// @EXPECT_ERROR: receives the loop element, but expected vector<integer>, got integer

struct S {
  n: integer,
}
fn vcap(x: integer, cap: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  (cap[0] ?? 0)+x
} // captured vector
fn scap(x: integer, s: S) -> integer {
  s.n + x
} // captured struct
fn misordered(first: vector<integer>, x: integer) -> integer {
  (first[0] ?? 0)+x
} // element not first

fn use_vcap() {
  c = [7]; for x in[1, 2] par(r = vcap(x, c), 2) {}
}
fn use_scap() {
  sv = S { n: 7 }; for x in[1, 2] par(r = scap(x, sv), 2) {}
}
fn use_mis() {
  c = [7]; for x in[1, 2] par(r = misordered(c, x), 2) {}
}

fn main() {}
